    {
      "id": "p0137-b009",
      "kind": "paragraph",
      "source": {
        "text": "In the English Undertale script, the bear says \"those two married dogs\" and \"those two\" when referring to Dogamy and Dogaressa, but how should they be counted in Japanese? The dogs walk and talk like people, which would warrant the counter word nin. But you can also pet them and play fetch with them like ordinary dogs, which would warrant hiki. So which should it be? And, perhaps more importantly, how would the Underground's denizens count them?",
        "raw": "In the English Undertale script, the bear says \"those two married dogs\" and\n\"those two\" when referring to Dogamy and Dogaressa, but how should they be\ncounted in Japanese? The dogs walk and talk like people, which would warrant\nthe counter word nin. But you can also pet them and play fetch with them like\nordinary dogs, which would warrant hiki. So which should it be? And, perhaps\nmore importantly, how would the Underground's denizens count them?",
        "status": "corrected",
        "languages": [
          "en"
        ],
        "bbox": [
          0.08655422,
          0.6742,
          0.70535668,
          0.77921429
        ],
        "mask_bbox": [
          0.0840247,
          0.67348571,
          0.70739661,
          0.78028571
        ],
        "line_ids": [
          34,
          35,
          36,
          37,
          38,
          39
        ]
      },
      "translation": {
        "text": "英文中，这只熊用“那对狗夫妻”和“那两个”指代 Dogamy 与 Dogaressa。日语该用哪个量词呢？它们像人一样走路、说话，似乎该用“人”；但它们又会让人抚摸、玩捡东西的游戏，似乎该用“匹”。更重要的是，地下世界的居民会怎样看待它们？",
        "status": "draft",
        "spans": []
      },
